# Star Bump - Unity 2D Game Project Cursor Rules

## Project Context
This is a Unity 2D physics-based game where missiles bounce off shields and interact with various game objects. The game uses Unity's Universal Render Pipeline (URP) and the new Input System.

## Documentation References
- Always refer to Unity version 6.1 docs
- Unity 2D Documentation: https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/Unity2D.html
- Unity Physics2D: https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/Physics2DReference.html
- Unity Input System: https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.inputsystem@1.7/manual/
- Unity URP: https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.render-pipelines.universal@latest/
- Unity ScriptableObjects: https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/class-ScriptableObject.html
- Unity Cinemachine: https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.cinemachine@latest/
- Unity Timeline: https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.timeline@latest/
- Unity Visual Scripting: https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.visualscripting@latest/
- C# Unity Scripting: https://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/

## Modern Unity Preferences (CRITICAL)
**Always prefer Unity's built-in, modern solutions over custom implementations:**

### Camera & Effects
- **PREFER**: Cinemachine Virtual Cameras over manual camera scripts
- **PREFER**: Cinemachine Impulse Sources for screenshake over custom transform manipulation
- **PREFER**: Cinemachine State-Driven Camera for camera transitions
- **PREFER**: Volume Framework for post-processing over manual effects
- **PREFER**: URP Renderer Features for custom rendering over OnRenderImage

### Animation & Timing
- **PREFER**: Unity Timeline for complex sequences over custom coroutines
- **PREFER**: Animation Events over polling in Update()
- **PREFER**: Animator State Machines over manual state management
- **PREFER**: DOTween (if available) or Unity's built-in Tweening over custom lerping
- **PREFER**: Unity's Job System for heavy computations over single-threaded code

### Audio
- **PREFER**: Audio Mixer Groups over direct AudioSource manipulation
- **PREFER**: Audio Snapshots for dynamic mixing over manual volume control
- **PREFER**: Spatial Audio settings over custom 3D audio calculations

### UI & Interface
- **PREFER**: UI Toolkit (UIElements) for editor tools over IMGUI
- **PREFER**: UI Toolkit for runtime UI in new projects
- **PREFER**: Event System and UnityEvents over direct method calls
- **PREFER**: Addressables for asset loading over Resources.Load()

### Input & Controls
- **PREFER**: Input System Actions over legacy Input class
- **PREFER**: Input System's Player Input component over manual input polling
- **PREFER**: Input System's UI Input Module for UI interaction

### Data Management
- **PREFER**: ScriptableObjects over static classes for game data
- **PREFER**: Unity Cloud Build for CI/CD over custom build scripts
- **PREFER**: Unity Analytics over custom analytics solutions
- **PREFER**: Unity's Localization Package over custom localization

### Performance & Optimization
- **PREFER**: Unity's Object Pooling over custom pool implementations
- **PREFER**: Unity Profiler modules over custom performance monitoring
- **PREFER**: Unity's Memory Profiler for memory optimization
- **PREFER**: GPU Instancing and Graphics.DrawMeshInstanced over individual renderers

## Unity-Specific Rules
- Always use Unity's new Input System (InputSystem_Actions.cs already exists)
- Prefer ScriptableObjects for data containers (like MissileData.cs)
- Use Unity's component-based architecture - favor composition over inheritance
- Follow Unity naming conventions: PascalCase for public fields, camelCase for private
- Use [SerializeField] for private fields that need Inspector exposure
- Always use RequireComponent attribute when components have dependencies
- Prefer GetComponent<>() in Awake() or Start(), cache references when possible

## Package Integration Guidelines
**When suggesting solutions, prioritize in this order:**
1. **Built-in Unity features** (no package needed)
2. **Official Unity packages** (Cinemachine, Timeline, Visual Scripting, etc.)
3. **Unity Registry packages** (verified by Unity)
4. **Custom implementations** (only when above options don't exist)

**Common Package Recommendations:**
- Cinemachine: For all camera work beyond basic following
- Timeline: For cutscenes, complex animations, and scripted sequences
- Addressables: For dynamic content loading and asset management
- Visual Scripting: For designers to create logic without coding
- Localization: For multi-language support
- Mathematics: For performance-critical math operations
- Collections: For high-performance data structures

## Physics & 2D Specific
- Use Physics2D methods for 2D physics (CircleCast, Raycast2D, etc.)
- Always account for object scale when working with collider radius calculations
- Use LayerMask for collision filtering
- Prefer Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity over the deprecated velocity property
- Use Physics2D.CircleCast for accurate collision prediction matching actual physics
- Prefer Physics2D.OverlapCircle over manual distance calculations
- Use ContactFilter2D for advanced collision filtering

## Code Style & Architecture
- Use meaningful variable names that describe the game context
- Group related functionality in separate classes (e.g., EnemySpawner, PlayerController)
- Use enums for game states and types (see Enums.cs)
- Implement Singleton pattern sparingly and only for true singletons
- Always null-check before destroying GameObjects
- Use coroutines for time-based operations and animations
- Prefer UnityEvents over direct method calls for loose coupling
- Use Unity's Logging system over Debug.Log for production builds

## Performance Considerations
- Cache frequently accessed components in Awake() or Start()
- Use object pooling for frequently instantiated objects (missiles, effects)
- Avoid expensive operations in Update() - prefer FixedUpdate() for physics
- Use CircleCast instead of multiple Raycasts when appropriate
- Prefer Unity's Job System for heavy calculations
- Use Unity Profiler to identify bottlenecks before optimizing
- Consider GPU Instancing for rendering many similar objects

## Modern Workflow Integration
- Use Unity's Package Manager for dependency management
- Leverage Unity Cloud Build for automated builds
- Use Version Control integration (Plastic SCM or Git)
- Prefer Unity's built-in testing framework for unit tests
- Use Unity's Analytics for player behavior insights
- Consider Unity's Multiplayer Netcode for networked features

## Game-Specific Context
- Missiles have colors and bounce off matching shield colors
- Trajectory drawing uses LineRenderer with physics prediction
- Enemy spawning should be configurable and scalable
- Player shield mechanics involve color matching and physics materials
- Visual effects (VFX) are used for missile explosions
- The game uses prefabs for reusable game objects
- Consider Cinemachine for camera shake effects during explosions
- Use Timeline for dramatic moments and boss introductions

## Error Handling
- Always check for null references before component access
- Use TryGetComponent<>() when component existence is uncertain
- Validate ScriptableObject data before use
- Handle edge cases in physics calculations (zero velocity, etc.)
- Use Unity's built-in exception handling and logging
- Implement graceful degradation for missing components

## Debugging & Maintenance
- Use Debug.Log sparingly in production code
- Prefer Unity's built-in profiler for performance analysis
- Comment complex physics calculations and trajectory logic
- Document public API methods and their expected behavior
- Use Unity's Visual Scripting for rapid prototyping
- Leverage Unity's built-in debugging tools (Frame Debugger, etc.)

## File Organization
- Scripts go in Assets/Scripts/ with appropriate subfolders
- Prefabs in Assets/Prefabs/
- ScriptableObjects in Assets/ScriptableObjects/
- Materials and Physics Materials in Assets/Materials/
- Timeline assets in Assets/Timeline/
- Cinemachine profiles in Assets/Cinemachine/
- Follow the existing folder structure consistently
- Use Assembly Definitions for large projects to improve compile times

## Solution Decision Matrix
**When asked to implement a feature, always consider this hierarchy:**

1. **Is there a built-in Unity solution?** → Use it
2. **Is there an official Unity package?** → Recommend it
3. **Is there a Unity Registry package?** → Consider it
4. **Does the feature require custom code?** → Implement with Unity best practices
5. **Would a third-party solution be better?** → Only if significantly superior

**Example Applications:**
- Screenshake → Cinemachine Impulse Source (not custom transform code)
- Audio mixing → Audio Mixer (not manual AudioSource control)
- Complex animations → Timeline + Cinemachine (not pure code)
- UI transitions → UI Toolkit animations (not custom tweening)
- Save system → ScriptableObjects + JsonUtility (not custom serialization)